cation against outcomes realized after the assessment; calibration of tail measures such as value-at-risk and expected shortfall, not just central predictive accuracy; comparisons against a null or naive benchmark to show the model beats a simple alternative; robustness to data vintage, since results are fitted on revised rather than real-time data; and demonstration that skill holds for the specific event and period being forecast rather than only in-sample or near-sample conditions."}, {"evidence_ids": ["7abd5499be5a9a5a842fb1da"], "kind": "interpretation", "text": "In short, useful out-of-sample forecasting is an empirical property of predictions compared with realized outcomes and suitable benchmarks; converged samplers are a necessary computational check but never sufficient evidence of predictive skill."}], "limitations": ["Sampler diagnostics for both model components pass, but convergence cannot demonstrate predictive skill.", "Holdout and hindcast validation is conditional on realized, latest-revised inputs and does not validate forecasts made under real-time climate outlook vintages.", "Rolling hindcasts were computed with a ridge challenger, not the Bayesian posterior used for the published risk estimates.", "No prospective verification against outcomes occurring after the assessment date is available.", "The models are conditional ENSO-response models without causal identification, so accuracy claims are conditional on the stated ENSO scenario and input assumptions."], "title": "Evidence needed for out-of-sample forecast claims"}}, "result": {"status": "ok", "data": {"passed": true, "errors": [], "semantic_review": "Descriptive and interpretive prose is not automatically scientifically validated."}}}
